  "summary": "Intermediate storage (K05: fusion) is the most likely performance bottleneck, followed by some wasted work from masking (K07), and potential suboptimal reduction parallelism (K06). Register/shared memory (K04), memory layout (K01), and launch overhead (K09) are unlikely limiting factors. No numerical/correctness (K10) or measurement (K12) issues detected."
}

{
  "plan": "Fuse the stats (mean+inv-std) and normalization into a single kernel, eliminating S (intermediate storage) and its global memory traffic. Each program computes mean and inv-std for its row, then applies normalization with per-column W/B in a second pass over the row. This avoids extra launches and storage, at the cost of higher register pressure. Use BLOCK=1024 for vectorization, mask for N=781, and preserve numerics and boundary masking per launch_contract. Launch one kernel: grid=[513], args=(X,W,B,Y,N,1024)."
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The fused layernorm kernel successfully eliminates the intermediate statistics buffer (S) and its associated global memory traffic, as intended. This results in a substantial reduction in logical memory bandwidth (from ~91 GB/s to ~176 GB/s) and nearly halves the kernel latency (median 0.034ms → 0.018ms). The transformation achieves its main goal: improved performance through fusion, with all outputs validated correct and no numerical or masking errors detected. Resource usage (registers, shared memory) increased as expected, but did not result in spills or occupancy issues for this problem size.",
  "outcome": "Fusion of statistics and normalization was highly effective, delivering a 1.87x confirmed speedup and removing the dominant bottleneck (intermediate memory traffic). All functional, numerical, and shape invariants passed validation. No further numerical or API issues were observed. The kernel now achieves close to the logical minimum traffic for this operation.",
  "next_step": "The primary bottleneck is resolved. Further gains may be possible by exploring increased intra-row parallelism (multi-warp reductions for rows with larger N), or by tuning BLOCK size for specific hardware. For very wide rows (N >> 1024), consider splitting reductions or employing more warps per row. For current N=781, the present configuration is near optimal. Focus next on other cases or hardware-specific tuning if required."
}

